1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:03,370
Maya: New AI‑native CRM challenges giants.

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:05,880
Maya: That, and fifteen more stories, on First Tick.

3
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,020
Alex: This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:18,760
Alex: What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:19,160
Alex: I'm Alex.

6
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:21,040
Maya: And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:25,360
Maya: It's Monday, November 16th, and this is First Tick: the day's tech,

8
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:30,000
Maya: AI and market news in about half an hour, with why each story matters.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,290
Maya: Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,420
Alex: On today's show.

11
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,640
Alex: New AI‑native CRM challenges giants.

12
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,540
Alex: Uber and Zoox hit the streets together.

13
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:43,550
Alex: Chips to China spark congressional fire.

14
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:46,420
Alex: Space launch disaster rocks ULA plans.

15
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:50,100
Alex: And Musk pushes for Nvidia CEO return.

16
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,460
Alex: Plus eleven more.

17
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:52,210
Alex: Let's get into it.

18
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:55,380
Maya: Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:55,380 --> 00:01:03,540
Maya: At Friday's close, the S&P 500 finished at 7,562, down 0.4 percent.

20
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:09,300
Maya: The Nasdaq Composite was down 0.9 percent and the Dow was up 0.1 percent.

21
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:13,480
Alex: And in big tech, Meta had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

22
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:18,090
Alex: while Nvidia was at the other end, down 2.0 percent.

23
00:01:18,090 --> 00:01:19,760
Alex: That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:24,440
Maya: They’re targeting Salesforce and HubSpot, says TechCrunch.

25
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:25,140
Alex: That fast?

26
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:29,000
Maya: The seed and Series A were led by Together Fund and Accel,

27
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,380
Maya: with Premji Invest in the mix.

28
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:34,670
Alex: Dreamteam says it auto‑captures email and call data,

29
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:39,110
Alex: then uses four AI agents to draft follow‑ups and monitor pipelines.

30
00:01:39,790 --> 00:01:42,690
Maya: They’ve got over 100 customers in three months,

31
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:45,660
Maya: and some have swapped Salesforce or HubSpot entirely.

32
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:52,590
Alex: A16z warned that AI will sit on top of existing CRMs, not replace them.

33
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:57,120
Maya: So the startup’s own CEO says the market is still open for a rethink,

34
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:58,320
Maya: not just feature add‑ons.

35
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:01,460
Maya: What actually powers that Company Brain?

36
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:03,540
Maya: How does it pull data from emails and calls?

37
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:06,220
Alex: It stitches together raw logs,

38
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:11,560
Alex: then feeds them into four specialized agents that can draft emails, flag risks,

39
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:12,500
Alex: and tweak workflows.

40
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:16,280
Maya: Those agents—are they all separate models or one monolith?

41
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,760
Alex: Four distinct ones, each tuned to a task,

42
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:22,460
Alex: but all sharing the same underlying memory pool.

43
00:02:22,942 --> 00:02:24,460
Maya: What about the funding split?

44
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:25,780
Maya: Did the seed and Series A differ?

45
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:30,110
Alex: Seed led by Together Fund, Series A by Accel,

46
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:35,080
Alex: with Premji Invest joining; the article keeps the exact amounts and valuation

47
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:35,660
Alex: hidden.

48
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:40,140
Maya: So no valuation reveal—does that hint at a cautious approach?

49
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:42,160
Alex: Could be; they’re still early,

50
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,540
Alex: and the market’s tight—maybe they’re keeping options open.

51
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:46,600
Alex: Wow, that speed?

52
00:02:47,010 --> 00:02:51,460
Maya: Yeah, 100 customers in three months is nothing to sneeze at,

53
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:53,100
Maya: but it’s a tiny slice of the market.

54
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:53,840
Alex: Even so?

55
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,000
Maya: True, but swapping a system of record at scale is a different beast.

56
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:02,100
Alex: If they’re pulling data from emails, they need a solid pipeline.

57
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:05,500
Maya: They say four agents share a memory pool,

58
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,820
Maya: but stitching that together is no small task.

59
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:11,840
Alex: I think the real win is automating follow‑ups and flagging risks.

60
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:15,140
Maya: So the market’s still open, but incumbents will adapt.

61
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:18,140
Maya: Did you see that?

62
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:22,100
Maya: Zoox robotaxis are now in the Uber app in Las Vegas, TechCrunch reports.

63
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:27,760
Alex: Yeah, it’s the first time Amazon‑owned Zoox teamed up with a ride‑hailing platform.

64
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:32,090
Maya: And Uber’s pulling in a second driverless partner after Waymo,

65
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:34,760
Maya: but they’re not giving Zoox the same maintenance support.

66
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:38,860
Alex: So Uber keeps the muscle on the tech side and just drops the cars.

67
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:41,860
Maya: They’re planning to keep it local for now,

68
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:46,580
Maya: with a multi‑year tie at MGM Resorts to help with pickups and signage.

69
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,480
Alex: I think that’s just a clever way to get a handle on the customer experience.

70
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:57,900
Maya: The plan is to expand supply later and maybe add a dedicated autonomous option when

71
00:03:57,900 --> 00:03:59,240
Maya: reliability improves.

72
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,780
Alex: Sounds like Uber is testing the waters before fully committing,

73
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:05,580
Alex: while Zoox gets a foot in the door.

74
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:07,640
Alex: One thousand robotaxis?

75
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:08,020
Alex: Seriously?

76
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:13,630
Maya: Nevada Transportation Authority approved Uber to deploy up to that many Zoox units

77
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:14,680
Maya: in Clark County.

78
00:04:14,772 --> 00:04:16,200
Alex: No direct selection yet.

79
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:20,600
Maya: Customers can tweak ride preferences to bump odds of getting a Zoox.

80
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:21,360
Alex: Hidden mode?

81
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:26,860
Maya: Uber plans a dedicated autonomous option once reliability improves,

82
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:27,900
Maya: maybe later this year.

83
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:28,780
Alex: MGM signage?

84
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:34,560
Maya: MGM Resorts will manage pickup zones and signage for Zoox robotaxis in their

85
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:34,960
Maya: properties.

86
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:39,940
Maya: So Uber is basically letting Zoox ride on their platform without maintenance—kind

87
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:40,680
Maya: of a free ride?

88
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:41,980
Alex: It's a test run.

89
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:44,600
Alex: They’re not putting their tech in the driver's seat.

90
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:49,640
Maya: But they’re still signing a multi-year deal with MGM; that feels like a strategic

91
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:50,160
Maya: foothold.

92
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:54,260
Alex: Maybe they just want the brand buzz, not the engineering headaches.

93
00:04:54,980 --> 00:05:00,100
Maya: I think the real win is for Uber to own more autonomous supply before Waymo drops

94
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:00,500
Maya: out.

95
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,920
Alex: I’m not convinced.

96
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:05,280
Alex: If reliability lags, customers will get frustrated.

97
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:10,910
Maya: Still, having a dedicated autonomous option eventually could tidy up the app.

98
00:05:11,750 --> 00:05:14,080
Alex: It sounds like a cautious rollout,

99
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:17,930
Alex: but the lack of maintenance support makes me wonder how long they’ll stick around.

100
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,840
Alex: Maya, did you hear the latest from The Verge about Nvidia chips?

101
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:24,780
Maya: Yeah, she fired the secretary.

102
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:28,100
Alex: She claims national security was undermined.

103
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:30,500
Maya: Companies tied to China got chips.

104
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:31,680
Alex: No way, big claim.

105
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:35,020
Maya: Letter cites clear congressional authority ignored.

106
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,280
Alex: Is the secretary being accused?

107
00:05:37,730 --> 00:05:39,440
Maya: Letter sent Sunday, says The Verge.

108
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,960
Alex: We’ll unpack its supply chain impact.

109
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,320
Maya: What’s the core of the letter’s claim?

110
00:05:44,710 --> 00:05:49,960
Alex: It says the Commerce Department has let AI chips slip into China through firms that

111
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:54,240
Alex: have business ties there, and that’s a breach of congressional authority.

112
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:54,980
Maya: No way.

113
00:05:55,430 --> 00:05:59,820
Alex: The letter specifically calls that a “national security” issue,

114
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:04,720
Alex: saying the flow of those chips “undermines America’s technology advantage.”

115
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:06,870
Maya: So the Secretary’s name is in it?

116
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:11,800
Alex: Yes, the letter is addressed to Secretary Howard Lutnick and accuses him of

117
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:15,620
Alex: neglecting the rules that are supposed to keep the chips out of China.

118
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:16,800
Maya: Any counter from the DOC?

119
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:21,640
Alex: No response yet, the article only reports the Senator’s accusations.

120
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:26,420
Alex: So basically the DOC just handed over some chips and shrugged.

121
00:06:26,930 --> 00:06:29,860
Maya: The letter says they let them slip through firms tied to China.

122
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:30,460
Alex: No way.

123
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:33,720
Maya: The Senator thinks it's a national‑security hit,

124
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:35,990
Maya: but the DOC hasn't even responded yet.

125
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:38,320
Alex: If the rules are clear, why the delay?

126
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:42,010
Maya: The letter says Congress gave the DOC the authority,

127
00:06:42,010 --> 00:06:43,700
Maya: so the Secretary’s in the hot seat.

128
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,640
Alex: Maybe it’s just a bureaucratic slip, not a chess move.

129
00:06:48,061 --> 00:06:52,140
Maya: The point is, the Senator wants to see enforcement, not just paperwork.

130
00:06:53,910 --> 00:06:58,900
Maya: A Vulcan rocket fell back after launch, taking 40 Amazon satellites with it.

131
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:00,060
Alex: That’s a pretty big hit.

132
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:05,300
Maya: The rocket was supposed to launch half of the US military's upcoming space missions.

133
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,240
Alex: Uh, so they’re grounded?

134
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:09,760
Maya: Yes, for an indefinite period, according to the report.

135
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,220
Alex: Sounds like a frequent fail.

136
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:16,440
Maya: It’s not the first close call; previous boosters had issues but the rocket still

137
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:17,100
Maya: reached orbit.

138
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:19,900
Alex: So they’ll review it before flying again.

139
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:24,310
Alex: How many of those Amazon satellites were in the payload, exactly?

140
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:29,720
Maya: Forty, all part of a 38-launch contract to roll out Amazon’s broadband

141
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:30,600
Maya: constellation.

142
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:35,420
Alex: So they’re still slated to launch the rest, or is the schedule on hold?

143
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:40,140
Maya: They’re on hold indefinitely; the failure triggers a full review before any flight.

144
00:07:40,225 --> 00:07:41,580
Alex: That review takes how long?

145
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:46,700
Maya: Months, but it’s already started; ULA had two earlier booster issues and still

146
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:48,500
Maya: launched on schedule after fixes.

147
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:52,520
Alex: Did ULA say why they kept going after the booster hiccups?

148
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:58,360
Maya: They mentioned “review and corrective actions” but no exact timeline; the report

149
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:00,200
Maya: just says they’ll resume when ready.

150
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:03,460
Maya: That review could drag on, months, maybe longer.

151
00:08:04,340 --> 00:08:06,200
Alex: Yeah, but ULA has bounced before.

152
00:08:06,670 --> 00:08:08,260
Maya: What about the $40 satellites?

153
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,040
Alex: 40 is just a number, but it's a 38‑launch deal.

154
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:14,300
Maya: So the whole constellation is on hold.

155
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:16,580
Alex: It sounds like a delay, not a halt.

156
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:19,100
Maya: The military’s payloads might wait, too.

157
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:23,600
Alex: I think the real issue is the engineering gap that keeps cropping up.

158
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:30,840
Alex: Musk, Replit, Epic and Y Combinator all signed a petition for Nvidia's CEO back.

159
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:35,980
Maya: They say Huang was removed without due process, and they want an independent probe.

160
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:36,640
Alex: No way.

161
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:41,840
Maya: Nvidia named CFO Colette Kress interim while they hunt a permanent replacement.

162
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:45,160
Maya: It sounds like a big fan club rallying for a CEO.

163
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,600
Alex: Hold on, is this just hype or genuine concern?

164
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:53,560
Maya: According to TechCrunch, the petition demands a fair investigation and more public

165
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:54,060
Maya: info.

166
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:56,160
Maya: What’s still unclear for our listeners?

167
00:08:56,520 --> 00:08:59,820
Maya: Wait, did you say 5,965 employees?

168
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:06,540
Alex: Yeah, and 1,108 former staff plus 63,063 shareholders.

169
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:09,100
Maya: That’s a mix of insiders and investors.

170
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:14,460
Alex: The petition says Huang was ousted without proper due process and wants an

171
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:15,540
Alex: independent probe.

172
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:17,640
Maya: So the board’s move is under fire.

173
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:22,490
Alex: With CFO Colette Kress stepping in, the stock took a dip,

174
00:09:22,490 --> 00:09:25,400
Alex: and people worry the AI funding gap will widen.

175
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:31,110
Alex: No way, a CEO rally for the board to reverse a decision that was already made.

176
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:35,140
Maya: It sounds like a campaign to pressure the board to bring him back while an

177
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:36,440
Maya: independent probe runs.

178
00:09:36,950 --> 00:09:42,200
Alex: Hold on, the petition says due process was missing, but that’s a political angle,

179
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:43,290
Alex: not a technical one.

180
00:09:43,630 --> 00:09:47,770
Maya: The board had to act on governance; they didn’t ignore due process,

181
00:09:47,770 --> 00:09:49,360
Maya: they chose an interim CEO.

182
00:09:50,410 --> 00:09:57,270
Alex: But the numbers—5,965 employees, 1,108 ex‑staff,

183
00:09:57,270 --> 00:10:02,060
Alex: 63,063 shareholders—show real internal support.

184
00:10:02,580 --> 00:10:06,320
Maya: That mix shows the board faces pressure from inside and outside,

185
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,340
Maya: not just a fan club.

186
00:10:07,870 --> 00:10:13,220
Alex: So my read is that this is more about optics and stakeholder confidence than actual

187
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:14,200
Alex: governance failure.

188
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:17,800
Maya: It is a sign that the board needs to explain its steps,

189
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:21,350
Maya: and the market will judge whether that transparency is enough.

190
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:26,340
Maya: Nvidia's CSO left August, CEO quits too.

191
00:10:26,890 --> 00:10:28,600
Alex: Wait, two departures in a few months?

192
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:29,200
Alex: That's…

193
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:29,640
Alex: weird.

194
00:10:30,050 --> 00:10:32,400
Maya: Reber said health reasons, Parkinson's.

195
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,170
Maya: He posted that.

196
00:10:33,590 --> 00:10:35,660
Alex: Health reason, then CEO…

197
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:36,580
Alex: relationships now?

198
00:10:36,890 --> 00:10:40,160
Maya: Huang linked to Megaspeed, and employee affairs.

199
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:41,350
Maya: TechCrunch cites reports.

200
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:44,940
Alex: Does the security team know about Megaspeed?

201
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:45,440
Alex: Unclear.

202
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:49,820
Maya: Security teams often underfunded, says ex‑intelligence officer.

203
00:10:50,750 --> 00:10:52,930
Alex: Yeah, that means they might miss everyday leaks.

204
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:56,840
Alex: What’s the real scoop on the new CSO, Saša Zdjelar?

205
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:02,560
Maya: He’s a former ExxonMobil senior IT manager, moved to Salesforce,

206
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:07,260
Maya: then ReversingLabs before Nvidia, but no word on his security briefings.

207
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:10,200
Alex: So no link to Megaspeed or Huang’s affairs?

208
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:12,100
Maya: None surfaced.

209
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:15,740
Maya: The only public note is that the lawyer was in talks with the government over

210
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:17,120
Maya: Megaspeed a year ago.

211
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:18,440
Alex: Lawyer talks, not security?

212
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:19,930
Maya: Exactly.

213
00:11:19,930 --> 00:11:24,100
Maya: The security team’s remit is narrow, and they get flagged as “compliance” only.

214
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,160
Alex: That paint a picture of a bubble‑filled org chart.

215
00:11:27,550 --> 00:11:30,720
Maya: And the ex‑intelligence officer said they’re underfunded,

216
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:32,460
Maya: so low‑level chatter slips by.

217
00:11:32,970 --> 00:11:35,340
Alex: So the leaks that happen every day just slip through?

218
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:40,880
Maya: It sounds like Nvidia just completely dropped the ball on security, honestly.

219
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:44,480
Maya: A lawyer talking to the government, but not the security team?

220
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:45,430
Maya: That’s…

221
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:45,900
Maya: concerning.

222
00:11:46,590 --> 00:11:47,920
Alex: Hold on, though.

223
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:50,120
Alex: It's not necessarily a dropped ball.

224
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:54,310
Alex: Maybe the lawyer’s involvement was standard procedure,

225
00:11:54,310 --> 00:11:58,260
Alex: and that's what they meant when they said they regularly contact officials.

226
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:03,000
Maya: Since when does “regular contact” with the government cover up personal

227
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,320
Maya: relationships and potential chip smuggling?

228
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:08,330
Maya: But if they should have been, Alex, that’s the problem.

229
00:12:08,330 --> 00:12:10,840
Maya: Underfunded, narrowly focused…

230
00:12:10,840 --> 00:12:12,680
Maya: that’s a recipe for missing the obvious.

231
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:13,960
Alex: Maybe.

232
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,960
Alex: But it’s also easy to armchair quarterback from the outside.

233
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:22,980
Alex: It’s a huge company, with complex layers of reporting and information flow—things

234
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:23,790
Alex: slip through.

235
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:25,290
Maya: I suppose.

236
00:12:25,290 --> 00:12:28,100
Maya: But the optics are terrible, regardless.

237
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:31,280
Maya: Two key departures, linked to serious issues…

238
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:33,360
Maya: it doesn't reflect well on the board’s oversight.

239
00:12:33,950 --> 00:12:39,030
Alex: It paints a picture of a company prioritizing growth over everything else,

240
00:12:39,030 --> 00:12:43,400
Alex: and that’s a pattern you see in a lot of tech—it’s a fragile foundation.

241
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:46,440
Alex: The biggest shock?

242
00:12:46,440 --> 00:12:50,380
Alex: 42% of AI projects get abandoned by their own parents.

243
00:12:50,380 --> 00:12:51,720
Maya: TechCrunch says that figure.

244
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:53,520
Alex: 42 percent?

245
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:55,040
Alex: That’s a lot of dead weight.

246
00:12:55,110 --> 00:12:55,740
Maya: Makes sense.

247
00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,380
Alex: What’s the most headline‑grabbing exit?

248
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,120
Maya: Character.ai went bankrupt, and Ollie shut down citing competition.

249
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,720
Alex: And OpenAI’s “super app” attempt?

250
00:13:06,130 --> 00:13:10,480
Maya: They rolled back a cluttered redesign and folded features into ChatGPT,

251
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:12,120
Maya: but still lost Sora.

252
00:13:12,580 --> 00:13:14,180
Maya: So what about the Apple delays, Alex?

253
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:18,500
Alex: They kept pushing Siri back because of bugs and engineering issues.

254
00:13:18,570 --> 00:13:19,300
Maya: That’s the usual.

255
00:13:19,840 --> 00:13:25,380
Alex: Yeah, and the $250 million settlement came after the iPhone 16 launch.

256
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:25,820
Maya: Oh?

257
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:26,780
Maya: What did Apple say?

258
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:30,740
Alex: They blamed the delay on technical hurdles, a typical excuse.

259
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:33,660
Maya: And Sora—why did it fail so fast?

260
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,240
Alex: High operating costs and low retention.

261
00:13:36,770 --> 00:13:38,530
Maya: It shows the market’s harsh test.

262
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:41,080
Alex: Character.ai and Ollie—both shut down.

263
00:13:41,130 --> 00:13:42,000
Maya: They couldn't keep up.

264
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:43,420
Alex: OpenAI's super app?

265
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:47,500
Maya: They folded it back into ChatGPT, but Sora died fast.

266
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:52,660
Alex: Apple keeps delaying Siri, then a $250 million settlement.

267
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:57,200
Maya: So the lesson is that hype alone won’t survive without solid demand.

268
00:13:58,830 --> 00:14:01,420
Maya: Alex, Nvidia's CEO spot—what's the buzz?

269
00:14:01,860 --> 00:14:03,780
Alex: Nvidia’s hiring, yeah.

270
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:04,980
Alex: They’re looking for a new head.

271
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:07,040
Maya: It’s not just a tech shift.

272
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:08,100
Maya: The market is watching.

273
00:14:08,690 --> 00:14:10,080
Alex: Hold on, what about the numbers?

274
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:15,780
Maya: Anthropic is eyeing a $1.4 trillion IPO, but that’s a side note.

275
00:14:16,330 --> 00:14:20,400
Alex: So they’re hiring while another AI firm is planning a huge IPO?

276
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:21,660
Maya: The timing feels tight.

277
00:14:22,230 --> 00:14:25,150
Alex: I think it’s a signal that the AI race is heating up.

278
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:26,660
Alex: Wait, who exactly’s filing?

279
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:27,580
Alex: And the numbers?

280
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:35,040
Maya: They’re targeting 825 million Class A shares, priced $70‑$78 each,

281
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:37,300
Maya: aiming for $64.3 billion.

282
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:38,640
Alex: That’s a big leap.

283
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:42,620
Maya: They also project a valuation up to $1.4 trillion.

284
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:44,820
Alex: So the IPO is a big deal.

285
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:50,060
Maya: Plus, the White House meeting with Speaker Johnson had tech execs on the table.

286
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:52,680
Maya: This feels like a chess move, not a scramble.

287
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:58,040
Alex: Hold on, what’s the point of a CEO hunt when the market’s already eyeing a

288
00:14:58,040 --> 00:14:59,360
Alex: trillion‑dollar IPO?

289
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:04,580
Maya: I think it signals confidence in AI revenue streams; the timing is just a

290
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:05,000
Maya: coincidence.

291
00:15:05,710 --> 00:15:09,320
Alex: I don't buy that; the timing suggests a tug‑of‑war for talent.

292
00:15:09,770 --> 00:15:13,450
Maya: The White House meeting shows regulators want to stay ahead,

293
00:15:13,450 --> 00:15:16,080
Maya: so Nvidia’s leadership change could smooth that.

294
00:15:16,567 --> 00:15:20,340
Alex: But if Anthropic is pulling $64 billion,

295
00:15:20,340 --> 00:15:23,180
Alex: Nvidia’s new CEO might just be a placeholder.

296
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:28,580
Maya: It could be a strategic hedge; keeping Nvidia stable while the IPO plays out.

297
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:33,860
Alex: In short, it’s a sign that the AI race isn’t just tech, it’s a market sprint.

298
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:38,530
Alex: You're listening to First Tick, from Bits and Tickers.

299
00:15:38,530 --> 00:15:40,270
Alex: That's about the halfway mark.

300
00:15:40,270 --> 00:15:41,040
Alex: Still to come.

301
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:44,980
Alex: AI debt surges, IMF warns.

302
00:15:44,980 --> 00:15:48,280
Alex: Nvidia earnings, a succession drama, and China.

303
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:51,540
Alex: And Uber picks up Amazon’s driverless cars in Vegas.

304
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:55,340
Maya: And if you just joined us, I'm Maya, that's Alex,

305
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:57,500
Maya: and every story is linked in the show notes.

306
00:15:59,090 --> 00:16:03,950
Alex: IMF says the AI market is basically a debt‑backed one‑legged stand.

307
00:16:04,390 --> 00:16:08,260
Maya: It’s $1 trillion, two‑thirds debt, according to CNBC.

308
00:16:08,652 --> 00:16:09,280
Alex: That fast?

309
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:11,270
Maya: Who’s pulling the strings on that debt?

310
00:16:11,470 --> 00:16:16,420
Alex: Investors keep buying AI companies, then sell back through orders, making a loop.

311
00:16:17,120 --> 00:16:22,310
Maya: And the risk is that a sudden pullback could knock $50 billion out, UBS notes.

312
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:27,020
Alex: If rates climb and borrowing costs rise, that loop could tighten quickly.

313
00:16:27,830 --> 00:16:31,260
Maya: So the market could wobble if confidence dips, IMF warns.

314
00:16:31,903 --> 00:16:35,780
Maya: What exactly do you mean by “circular financing” in the AI space?

315
00:16:36,140 --> 00:16:41,240
Alex: It’s when money poured into an AI firm ends up flowing back when that firm places

316
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:46,680
Alex: orders with suppliers—so the investor’s cash circles back through the supply chain.

317
00:16:47,230 --> 00:16:49,660
Maya: So it’s not a straight line of funding, it loops back?

318
00:16:50,030 --> 00:16:53,920
Alex: And the IMF says that whole loop now totals just over $1 trillion.

319
00:16:54,850 --> 00:16:57,960
Maya: Two‑thirds of that is debt—do we know why it’s so leveraged?

320
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:03,800
Alex: Investors are chasing quick returns; debt lets them amplify exposure without

321
00:17:03,800 --> 00:17:05,670
Alex: putting more equity on the table.

322
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:08,090
Maya: But who’s the main debt‑lender in this loop?

323
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:14,200
Alex: The article points to Nvidia’s financing pullback as a big threat—UBS estimates a

324
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:17,230
Alex: $50 billion hit if Nvidia pulls back.

325
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:22,680
Alex: I think the debt loop is a fragile trampoline; one misstep and the whole thing

326
00:17:22,680 --> 00:17:23,960
Alex: could bounce out of shape.

327
00:17:24,780 --> 00:17:28,210
Maya: Yeah, but the IMF says it’s “orderly” right now,

328
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:30,730
Maya: so maybe it’s just a shaky platform that’s holding up.

329
00:17:31,270 --> 00:17:35,180
Alex: The loop’s already over a trillion—more than a typical startup round.

330
00:17:35,630 --> 00:17:38,220
Maya: It’s debt‑heavy, so if the interest rates climb,

331
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:41,000
Maya: the cost of keeping that loop alive spikes.

332
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:45,060
Alex: So the pullback from Nvidia could yank $50 billion?

333
00:17:45,060 --> 00:17:47,220
Alex: That’s a lot of debt suddenly gone.

334
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:52,160
Maya: My read is that the market is watching the AI leg; if confidence dips,

335
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:54,130
Maya: the whole supply chain feels it.

336
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:59,680
Alex: It’s like building a house on a single beam; if one bolt loosens, the roof tilts.

337
00:18:00,030 --> 00:18:03,880
Maya: So the takeaway is, keep an eye on the debt load—if it wobbles,

338
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:05,570
Maya: the whole economy might feel the shake.

339
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:11,580
Maya: This earnings report caught my eye because it’s the first one since Jensen Huang

340
00:18:11,580 --> 00:18:13,920
Maya: stepped down, according to CNBC.

341
00:18:13,920 --> 00:18:15,020
Alex: Stepped down?

342
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:17,870
Maya: Yeah, he resigned last Thursday,

343
00:18:17,870 --> 00:18:21,820
Maya: and Colette Kress is now interim CEO while they look for a replacement.

344
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:22,880
Alex: Okay, that's new.

345
00:18:23,410 --> 00:18:25,740
Maya: They're expecting significant growth,

346
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:31,280
Maya: still – 91% revenue growth is the current estimate – but the real questions are

347
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:35,840
Maya: about China, deals with cloud providers, and what the future looks like.

348
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:37,600
Alex: The stock’s been tanking, huh?

349
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:42,900
Maya: Six weeks straight, the longest losing streak since 2012.

350
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:47,110
Maya: Nvidia’s pushing back on some of the concerns about their China sales,

351
00:18:47,110 --> 00:18:49,460
Maya: saying they haven't been contacted by the government.

352
00:18:49,900 --> 00:18:51,920
Alex: Hold on, what’s the story with Megaspeed?

353
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:57,540
Maya: Apparently, there are claims about Huang's connections to a Megaspeed executive and

354
00:18:57,540 --> 00:18:59,880
Maya: potential diversion of chips.

355
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:01,940
Maya: They're doing an internal investigation.

356
00:19:02,550 --> 00:19:04,220
Alex: What about the China sales claims?

357
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:11,460
Maya: They say 20% of 2026 compute revenue was driven by China, per a short‑seller note.

358
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:13,530
Alex: So the numbers are in the mix.

359
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:18,580
Maya: The company denies any diversion, saying no evidence of illegal activity.

360
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:19,680
Alex: And the Megaspeed probe?

361
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:25,180
Maya: Megaspeed says it's doing a full internal review; the exec linked to Alice Huang

362
00:19:25,180 --> 00:19:27,220
Maya: left in 2025.

363
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:28,420
Alex: That’s a tight timeline.

364
00:19:28,940 --> 00:19:33,220
Maya: Kress has to walk that tightrope while answering the board’s questions on supply

365
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:34,800
Maya: constraints and future growth.

366
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:39,720
Maya: I think it’s pretty clear that the market isn’t buying the denials about China.

367
00:19:39,720 --> 00:19:42,560
Maya: Six weeks of losses is a reaction, Alex.

368
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:43,980
Alex: It is.

369
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:48,620
Alex: But the stock dropping doesn't automatically mean they’re guilty of anything.

370
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:50,280
Alex: It just means people are nervous.

371
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:55,480
Maya: Nervous enough to wipe out $1.7 trillion in market cap.

372
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:56,640
Maya: That’s a lot of nervousness.

373
00:19:57,110 --> 00:19:59,760
Alex: Still, the Megaspeed investigation…

374
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:01,420
Alex: that’s the interesting part, I think.

375
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:02,640
Maya: Really?

376
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:06,960
Maya: I’m more concerned about the potential revenue impact if those China sales were

377
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:07,400
Maya: diverted.

378
00:20:07,980 --> 00:20:10,240
Alex: Diversion is a big claim.

379
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:14,340
Alex: And frankly, it sounds like Megaspeed is trying to look proactive,

380
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:16,200
Alex: even if they’re just scrambling now.

381
00:20:16,990 --> 00:20:21,660
Maya: But Kress has a tough job ahead of her - she needs to reassure everyone and somehow

382
00:20:21,660 --> 00:20:25,700
Maya: steer growth projections forward, all while navigating this mess.

383
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:29,910
Alex: It seems like they’re building a narrative around compliance,

384
00:20:29,910 --> 00:20:34,340
Alex: but I suspect the underlying questions about their distribution network aren’t

385
00:20:34,340 --> 00:20:36,280
Alex: going away anytime soon.

386
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:40,980
Alex: Uber’s now handing us Zoox rides, no extra cost.

387
00:20:41,500 --> 00:20:42,540
Maya: That’s from the Verge, right?

388
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:45,460
Alex: Yeah, they’re opening their fleet to a third‑party app.

389
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:49,000
Maya: Uber’s still partnering with Waymo, but Zoox is new.

390
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:52,070
Alex: They’re only covering Strip, downtown, Arts District.

391
00:20:52,650 --> 00:20:54,720
Maya: Makes sense, those are tourist hotspots.

392
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:58,300
Alex: Zoox keeps its own app too—won’t it split the market?

393
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:02,540
Maya: They’re also tweaking pickup zones with MGM to avoid chaos.

394
00:21:03,260 --> 00:21:05,140
Maya: What’s the deal with the MGM partnership?

395
00:21:05,730 --> 00:21:11,160
Alex: They’re putting in custom wayfinding signs and app prompts so the pods can actually

396
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:14,340
Alex: pick people up at the Bellagio and MGM Grand.

397
00:21:14,550 --> 00:21:15,160
Maya: That sounds…

398
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:16,000
Maya: complicated.

399
00:21:16,560 --> 00:21:19,700
Alex: The resorts are turning into a maze of valets, tour buses,

400
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:22,550
Alex: and tourists—hard for an AI to parse.

401
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,990
Maya: And they’re still keeping Zoox’s own app?

402
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:29,380
Alex: They’re not abandoning it; they’ll run the pods both through Uber and their

403
00:21:29,380 --> 00:21:30,540
Alex: standalone platform.

404
00:21:30,700 --> 00:21:31,820
Maya: So future LA launch?

405
00:21:32,300 --> 00:21:38,760
Alex: Zoox plans to debut in Los Angeles by the end of 2026 with Uber integration

406
00:21:38,760 --> 00:21:41,160
Alex: following in 2027.

407
00:21:41,610 --> 00:21:45,290
Alex: That partnership feels like a safety net for Uber's brand,

408
00:21:45,290 --> 00:21:48,180
Alex: but it also means more layers of tech we have to trust.

409
00:21:48,560 --> 00:21:52,900
Maya: But the fact that Zoox keeps its own app shows they’re not fully committed to

410
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:53,840
Maya: Uber’s ecosystem.

411
00:21:54,540 --> 00:21:57,100
Alex: Makes sense that they want to keep their own fan base.

412
00:21:57,750 --> 00:22:00,630
Maya: So, Uber is just a conduit now, not the builder.

413
00:22:01,140 --> 00:22:05,980
Alex: Exactly—Uber’s platform is becoming the playground for anyone who can drive a car.

414
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:11,090
Maya: Hold on, that could dilute the market; if Zoox can pull riders off Uber,

415
00:22:11,090 --> 00:22:12,460
Maya: the partnership might backfire.

416
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:17,800
Alex: I think the MGM tweaks are clever—clear signage and app prompts could actually

417
00:22:17,800 --> 00:22:18,660
Alex: reduce chaos.

418
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:22,610
Maya: It sounds like a partnership that could smooth the ride,

419
00:22:22,610 --> 00:22:24,360
Maya: but only if the tech adapts quickly.

420
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,420
Maya: Apparently, Jensen Huang stepped down.

421
00:22:28,420 --> 00:22:29,480
Maya: That's the biggest thing, right?

422
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:29,940
Alex: Huh.

423
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:30,740
Alex: Didn’t see that coming.

424
00:22:31,330 --> 00:22:36,690
Maya: The news broke Friday and the stock is still dropping, down almost 4% now,

425
00:22:36,690 --> 00:22:37,820
Maya: according to CNBC.

426
00:22:38,164 --> 00:22:39,710
Alex: A petition to reinstate him?

427
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,820
Maya: It already has over 170,000 signatures.

428
00:22:43,450 --> 00:22:45,440
Alex: Hold on, what’s the reason he resigned?

429
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:50,300
Maya: It was reported he had romantic relationships with employees.

430
00:22:50,300 --> 00:22:51,520
Maya: Nvidia hasn’t commented.

431
00:22:52,110 --> 00:22:56,040
Alex: And now Treasury Secretary Bessent wants a “dramatic shakeup” too?

432
00:22:56,420 --> 00:22:58,680
Alex: What’s the next thing people’re still hanging on to?

433
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:03,780
Maya: Colette Kress, the finance chief, stepped in as interim CEO,

434
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:08,540
Maya: and the board said they’re weighing both inside and outside candidates—so it’s not

435
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:10,230
Maya: just a quick fix.

436
00:23:10,230 --> 00:23:14,800
Maya: The Treasury Secretary called for a “dramatic shakeup” and even said other Nvidia

437
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:18,300
Maya: executives should be examined for export‑control compliance.

438
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:19,360
Alex: That’s a lot of drama.

439
00:23:19,890 --> 00:23:23,830
Maya: The petition got over 170,000 signatures,

440
00:23:23,830 --> 00:23:27,140
Maya: and tech leaders like Elon Musk joined the chorus,

441
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:29,500
Maya: which shows the rally isn’t just a fringe thing.

442
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:31,840
Alex: So the market’s feeling the heat?

443
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:32,880
Alex: Wow, that’s a hit.

444
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:38,360
Maya: It’s a sign the board’s in a tough spot; the next CEO choice will shape whether

445
00:23:38,360 --> 00:23:39,960
Maya: investors stay or pull back.

446
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:43,060
Maya: I think the petition is a telling sign.

447
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:47,880
Maya: It shows how much people tied Nvidia’s success to Huang, and now they’re panicking.

448
00:23:48,340 --> 00:23:49,400
Alex: That's wild.

449
00:23:49,400 --> 00:23:51,930
Alex: People really latch on to personalities, don't they?

450
00:23:52,230 --> 00:23:54,800
Maya: It’s more than just personality, Alex.

451
00:23:54,800 --> 00:23:57,250
Maya: It's about stability and a track record.

452
00:23:57,250 --> 00:23:59,720
Maya: The board is in a really awkward position now,

453
00:23:59,720 --> 00:24:02,420
Maya: needing to find someone who can reassure the market.

454
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:05,900
Alex: Maybe the petition is just performative.

455
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:08,770
Alex: People online say a lot of things, right?

456
00:24:08,770 --> 00:24:12,540
Alex: It doesn’t necessarily reflect what actual investors are doing.

457
00:24:13,160 --> 00:24:16,520
Maya: Maybe, but the stock drop itself is undeniable.

458
00:24:16,520 --> 00:24:20,020
Maya: That’s a tangible reaction, and it’s got to be worrying for the board.

459
00:24:20,640 --> 00:24:25,760
Alex: I think Bessent’s call for a "dramatic shakeup" is the most interesting part.

460
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:29,880
Alex: That’s not something you hear every day, particularly someone in his position.

461
00:24:30,290 --> 00:24:34,500
Maya: It’s a clear indication that they need to be looking outside the company,

462
00:24:34,500 --> 00:24:38,130
Maya: which suggests they don't see an obvious internal candidate.

463
00:24:38,470 --> 00:24:42,600
Alex: Ultimately, Nvidia built its reputation on execution,

464
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:46,740
Alex: and they need someone who can continue that, regardless of who’s leading.

465
00:24:48,310 --> 00:24:50,810
Alex: Maya, have you heard the Vulcan launch fizzled out?

466
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:53,880
Maya: Yeah, 10 minutes in, it stalled and fell apart.

467
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:55,140
Alex: So it never reached orbit?

468
00:24:55,700 --> 00:24:58,740
Maya: Exactly, 40 Amazon Leo satellites were lost.

469
00:24:59,310 --> 00:25:01,930
Alex: That’s the first failure in ULA’s 20‑year run.

470
00:25:02,460 --> 00:25:05,600
Maya: ULA said the cause is still unclear, but they’ll investigate.

471
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:07,000
Alex: Amazon’s still confident.

472
00:25:07,530 --> 00:25:10,780
Maya: They posted on X that risk is normal, and they trust ULA.

473
00:25:11,240 --> 00:25:14,680
Maya: I wonder, for those who are still wrapping their heads around this,

474
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:16,940
Maya: what exactly is an ‘anomaly’ in this context?

475
00:25:17,480 --> 00:25:21,040
Alex: Apparently, it's what ULA is calling it for now.

476
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:23,210
Alex: They haven't pinpointed the root cause,

477
00:25:23,210 --> 00:25:27,740
Alex: but it happened about ten minutes after launch, from Space Launch Complex 41.

478
00:25:28,490 --> 00:25:29,050
Maya: Right.

479
00:25:29,050 --> 00:25:32,010
Maya: And Amazon bought 38 more of these launches, didn’t they?

480
00:25:32,215 --> 00:25:32,530
Maya: Huh.

481
00:25:32,900 --> 00:25:34,720
Alex: They’re putting a brave face on it,

482
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:38,070
Alex: saying it’s part of the risk with inaugural missions.

483
00:25:38,070 --> 00:25:40,760
Alex: It sounds like they’re trying to reassure investors.

484
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:41,940
Maya: I see.

485
00:25:41,940 --> 00:25:45,640
Maya: So, just to be clear, the statement came from ULA’s X account?

486
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:49,200
Alex: Yep, and they say they’ll be transparent with their investigation.

487
00:25:49,910 --> 00:25:54,880
Alex: So ULA lost all 40 Leo sats—first failure in two decades, huh?

488
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:55,380
Maya: A clean wipe.

489
00:25:55,501 --> 00:25:57,420
Alex: Amazon still buying 38 more?

490
00:25:57,740 --> 00:25:59,140
Maya: They’re betting on learning from this.

491
00:25:59,700 --> 00:26:01,930
Alex: I think it shows a bigger risk for any launch.

492
00:26:02,830 --> 00:26:05,340
Maya: And that’s exactly why they’re saying risk is normal.

493
00:26:05,900 --> 00:26:09,140
Alex: But if the anomaly’s unclear, how can they promise success?

494
00:26:09,620 --> 00:26:12,970
Maya: My read is that they’re just keeping investors calm while they dig in.

495
00:26:14,480 --> 00:26:19,140
Maya: Apparently, there's a movement to reinstate Jensen Huang as Nvidia’s CEO.

496
00:26:19,348 --> 00:26:19,740
Alex: Seriously?

497
00:26:19,740 --> 00:26:25,540
Maya: According to CNBC, a petition has almost 180,000 signatures,

498
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:28,770
Maya: and people like Musk and Sweeney have signed it.

499
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:31,040
Alex: Hold on, why would they want that?

500
00:26:31,400 --> 00:26:35,320
Maya: It follows his resignation last week, after reports about...

501
00:26:35,320 --> 00:26:37,860
Maya: well, let's just say some personal matters surfaced.

502
00:26:38,370 --> 00:26:41,280
Alex: Right, and now everyone’s a public relations expert.

503
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:44,060
Maya: The board is looking for replacements,

504
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:48,850
Maya: but shareholders like Brad Gerstner are urging them to consider Huang's return,

505
00:26:48,850 --> 00:26:50,740
Maya: saying he deserves a fair process.

506
00:26:50,740 --> 00:26:51,320
Alex: Says who?

507
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:54,060
Alex: What’s the board doing while the petition swells?

508
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:57,940
Maya: They’re still vetting candidates, even Colette Kress is interim.

509
00:26:58,410 --> 00:26:58,960
Alex: Colette?

510
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:00,840
Alex: She’s the finance chief, not a tech guy.

511
00:27:01,420 --> 00:27:03,780
Maya: Yeah, but she’s the one steering the search right now.

512
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:05,560
Alex: So no one’s pulling the trigger?

513
00:27:06,030 --> 00:27:08,370
Maya: The board’s keeping options open,

514
00:27:08,370 --> 00:27:12,680
Maya: but they’re also under pressure from investors like Brad Gerstner to be fair.

515
00:27:12,680 --> 00:27:13,240
Alex: Fair how?

516
00:27:13,621 --> 00:27:17,480
Maya: They’re calling for an independent probe into Huang’s exit and a transparent

517
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:19,720
Maya: process before picking a new CEO.

518
00:27:20,350 --> 00:27:23,600
Maya: Well, the board is still hunting, but the petition keeps growing.

519
00:27:24,010 --> 00:27:27,720
Alex: Seriously, a petition of 180,000?

520
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:29,920
Alex: That’s a lot of noise for a corporate board.

521
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:34,960
Maya: It shows shareholders like Gerstner feel the board should give Huang a fair process

522
00:27:34,960 --> 00:27:35,840
Maya: before deciding.

523
00:27:36,820 --> 00:27:40,860
Alex: Fair process or not, the board still needs to vet candidates,

524
00:27:40,860 --> 00:27:42,740
Alex: and Colette is steering that search.

525
00:27:43,290 --> 00:27:47,590
Maya: The pressure from investors could push the board toward a quick decision,

526
00:27:47,590 --> 00:27:51,220
Maya: but they’re also under scrutiny for how they handle the investigation.

527
00:27:51,870 --> 00:27:56,580
Alex: And we’re not even sure if the petition reflects real employee sentiment,

528
00:27:56,580 --> 00:27:58,540
Alex: since those claims aren’t verified.

529
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:02,530
Maya: Still, it signals a loyalty to the founder’s vision,

530
00:28:02,530 --> 00:28:04,240
Maya: and maybe that matters more than the hype.

531
00:28:04,810 --> 00:28:09,080
Alex: My read is that the board will have to balance the petition’s weight with the need

532
00:28:09,080 --> 00:28:15,490
Alex: for a transparent, independent probe before anyone steps back into the CEO role.

533
00:28:17,080 --> 00:28:21,240
Alex: Amazon wants drones in 500 towns by 2026.

534
00:28:22,500 --> 00:28:25,820
Maya: DoorDash, Zipline, Uber, Walmart all in the game too.

535
00:28:26,256 --> 00:28:27,880
Alex: Huh, that’s a lot of flying bots.

536
00:28:28,370 --> 00:28:31,280
Maya: Dronophobia is a real thing, PTSD linked.

537
00:28:31,830 --> 00:28:34,640
Alex: War footage fuels that fear, not just delivery.

538
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:37,800
Maya: FAA’s line-of-sight rules are still tightening.

539
00:28:38,360 --> 00:28:40,400
Alex: So they’re moving beyond line-of-sight now.

540
00:28:40,780 --> 00:28:43,180
Maya: That’s the next hurdle, but the anxiety stays.

541
00:28:43,800 --> 00:28:47,080
Maya: What about the tech side—how do these drones actually drop stuff?

542
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:50,820
Alex: They use a little chute that lowers a bag via a winch.

543
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:53,690
Alex: The chute opens when it hits a curb or a lawn edge.

544
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:56,420
Maya: So they’re just like paratroopers for packages?

545
00:28:56,960 --> 00:28:58,100
Maya: And the FAA?

546
00:28:58,100 --> 00:28:59,120
Maya: Still tightening rules?

547
00:28:59,600 --> 00:29:02,080
Alex: Yeah, the line‑of‑sight rule is easing,

548
00:29:02,080 --> 00:29:05,850
Alex: but there’s a new waiver process that pilots need to file.

549
00:29:05,850 --> 00:29:07,040
Alex: It’s a slow rollout.

550
00:29:07,210 --> 00:29:08,160
Maya: Hold on, who’s filing?

551
00:29:08,610 --> 00:29:14,320
Alex: Amazon, DoorDash, Zipline—each has its own compliance team pushing the paperwork.

552
00:29:15,220 --> 00:29:19,420
Maya: Got it, so the anxiety stays but the tech is getting easier to deploy.

553
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:21,600
Alex: Feels like a sci‑fi plot, huh.

554
00:29:22,120 --> 00:29:23,860
Maya: But the money's already on that side.

555
00:29:24,450 --> 00:29:27,560
Alex: Still, dropping a bag on a lawn—simple tech, but safety?

556
00:29:28,020 --> 00:29:30,880
Maya: Safety’s fine, but the anxiety keeps rising.

557
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:32,220
Alex: Drones = PTSD?

558
00:29:32,860 --> 00:29:34,340
Maya: The footage fuels that, no doubt.

559
00:29:34,990 --> 00:29:36,920
Alex: So we’re trading snack drops for nightmares.

560
00:29:37,700 --> 00:29:39,380
Maya: That's the price we pay for convenience.

561
00:29:41,180 --> 00:29:43,940
Maya: Nvidia shares dip premarket, CNBC says.

562
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:45,560
Alex: No way, Huang talks return?

563
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:49,700
Maya: Financial Times reports he discussed a comeback with confidants.

564
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:51,200
Alex: So the board’s in turmoil?

565
00:29:51,620 --> 00:29:55,260
Maya: Cantor Fitzgerald’s share count cut by half, CNBC notes.

566
00:29:55,880 --> 00:29:58,780
Alex: That’s a big change for a brokerage tied to Nvidia.

567
00:29:59,330 --> 00:30:05,360
Maya: And the stock’s longest weekly loss since 2012, market value down $1.7 trillion.

568
00:30:06,670 --> 00:30:08,380
Alex: What’s the takeaway for the tech scene?

569
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:11,640
Alex: So, what’s driving these reinstatement calls?

570
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:13,480
Alex: It’s not just about sentiment, is it?

571
00:30:13,710 --> 00:30:16,280
Maya: Apparently, it's about the perceived value,

572
00:30:16,280 --> 00:30:20,870
Maya: which they're calling the "Jensen premium." The article doesn’t really explain what

573
00:30:20,870 --> 00:30:23,100
Maya: that is, but it’s clearly being debated.

574
00:30:23,245 --> 00:30:25,340
Alex: A billion dollars on GTA VI?

575
00:30:25,970 --> 00:30:28,930
Maya: Take-Two Interactive spent over a billion developing it,

576
00:30:28,930 --> 00:30:31,800
Maya: according to Roth analyst Eric Handler.

577
00:30:31,800 --> 00:30:36,190
Maya: And preorders are already through the roof, twelve million through November 13th.

578
00:30:36,780 --> 00:30:40,480
Alex: Hold on, what does the summit in Shenzhen have to do with any of this?

579
00:30:40,840 --> 00:30:44,910
Maya: It's a meeting between Xi Jinping, Putin, and Trump.

580
00:30:44,910 --> 00:30:47,290
Maya: It’s described as unprecedented,

581
00:30:47,290 --> 00:30:50,080
Maya: following multiple bilateral meetings over the years.

582
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:53,130
Alex: Just seems like a lot of noise all at once.

583
00:30:53,440 --> 00:30:59,220
Maya: It is; Luke Fountain at CNBC says it’s a troubled time for the tech industry ahead

584
00:30:59,220 --> 00:31:01,140
Maya: of the Nasdaq debut.

585
00:31:01,140 --> 00:31:02,700
Maya: It’s all about the stakes being high.

586
00:31:03,230 --> 00:31:05,860
Maya: The Jensen premium debate is heating up,

587
00:31:05,860 --> 00:31:08,700
Maya: and people keep saying it’s the reason for the CEO chatter.

588
00:31:08,900 --> 00:31:09,340
Alex: No way.

589
00:31:09,850 --> 00:31:13,340
Maya: If the board is split, maybe it’s just a power play, not a tech issue.

590
00:31:13,790 --> 00:31:17,160
Alex: The board’s split shows the chips are more fragile than the hardware.

591
00:31:17,930 --> 00:31:21,830
Maya: Does the half‑cut at Cantor Fitzgerald mean investors are pulling back?

592
00:31:22,260 --> 00:31:27,220
Alex: It looks like a big shift in ownership, but the article just reports the drop,

593
00:31:27,220 --> 00:31:27,940
Alex: no analysis.

594
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:31,760
Maya: And GTA VI is already breaking pre‑order records!

595
00:31:32,220 --> 00:31:37,240
Alex: In the end, it’s a reminder that even the biggest names can get tangled in politics

596
00:31:37,240 --> 00:31:40,360
Alex: and numbers, and the market keeps moving regardless.

597
00:31:42,220 --> 00:31:43,300
Alex: That's the news.

598
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:44,120
Alex: Time for The Takeaway.

599
00:31:44,750 --> 00:31:48,960
Maya: I think the biggest thread connecting all this chaos is just how quickly things can

600
00:31:48,960 --> 00:31:53,920
Maya: shift in the AI space—a rocket failure, a CEO stepping down,

601
00:31:53,920 --> 00:31:57,460
Maya: a trillion dollars circling—it's all moving so fast.

602
00:31:57,780 --> 00:31:58,040
Alex: Yeah.

603
00:31:58,040 --> 00:32:02,100
Alex: It’s a stark reminder that building anything real is hard,

604
00:32:02,100 --> 00:32:04,650
Alex: regardless of how much hype there is.

605
00:32:04,650 --> 00:32:06,700
Alex: Makes you wonder what's genuinely solid.

606
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:12,280
Maya: I still think Nvidia's stock reaction is understated; the market is pricing in

607
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:13,540
Maya: something pretty significant.

608
00:32:13,540 --> 00:32:17,040
Maya: I’ll be watching Colette Kress’s next moves closely.

609
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:22,840
Alex: I’m curious to see how the drone PTSD story develops—it's a valid concern,

610
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:25,820
Alex: and could genuinely impact adoption.

611
00:32:25,820 --> 00:32:26,340
Alex: It’s just…

612
00:32:26,340 --> 00:32:27,300
Alex: unexpected.

613
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:31,340
Maya: Hold on, you’re focusing on the niche concerns.

614
00:32:31,340 --> 00:32:36,000
Maya: My take is that Nvidia’s reinstatement calls are just pure market signaling,

615
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:38,040
Maya: a bid to prop up a wobbling share price.

616
00:32:38,650 --> 00:32:40,880
Alex: I don't disagree, but a petition from Musk?

617
00:32:40,880 --> 00:32:44,110
Alex: That’s more than just simple signaling, isn’t it?

618
00:32:44,110 --> 00:32:44,534
Alex: It’s…

619
00:32:45,160 --> 00:32:48,100
Maya: I think it’s a performative gesture, Alex.

620
00:32:48,100 --> 00:32:51,300
Maya: I remain convinced the market will ultimately dictate the outcome.

621
00:32:51,780 --> 00:32:56,080
Alex: Well, I’m going to keep an eye on Vulcan's next attempt—it's a fascinating,

622
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:59,140
Alex: and arguably vital, alternative to SpaceX.

623
00:33:01,050 --> 00:33:02,380
Maya: And that's First Tick.

624
00:33:02,380 --> 00:33:05,660
Maya: Every story we talked about is linked in the show notes.

625
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:07,100
Maya: Got a question for us?

626
00:33:07,100 --> 00:33:08,540
Maya: The link to send it is there too.

627
00:33:09,020 --> 00:33:10,720
Alex: Next up is First Tick, tomorrow.

628
00:33:10,720 --> 00:33:16,420
Alex: Two reminders: our voices are made with AI, and nothing here is financial advice.

629
00:33:16,420 --> 00:33:18,900
Alex: From Bits and Tickers, I'm Alex.

630
00:33:18,900 --> 00:33:19,960
Alex: Keep building.

631
00:33:20,012 --> 00:33:20,500
Maya: I'm Maya.

632
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:21,520
Maya: And keep counting.
